<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="lt1-order-of-operations-v2">
  <title>Learning Target 1: Order of Operations</title>
  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>I can properly apply the order of operations to evaluate a numerical expression.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <!-- ============================================================
       PROBLEM 1: Evaluating Expressions (Similar Questions)
       ============================================================ -->
 
    <exercise xml:id="evaluating-expressions-similar">
      <title>Evaluating Expressions</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Evaluate the following expressions step-by-step without using an electronic tool. Be sure to treat the fraction bar as a grouping symbol where appropriate. Show all intermediate steps to illustrate your reasoning.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task workspace="2.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <me>\frac{4(5-2)^2}{2^3 + 1}</me>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Following the order of operations, treat the numerator and denominator as separate groups defined by the fraction bar.
          </p>
          <p>
            First, simplify the expression inside the parentheses in the numerator:
            <me>\frac{4(3)^2}{2^3 + 1}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Next, evaluate the exponents in both the numerator and the denominator:
            <me>\frac{4(9)}{8 + 1}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Perform the multiplication in the numerator and the addition in the denominator:
            <me>\frac{36}{9}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, divide the numerator by the denominator:
            <me>4</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>

      <task workspace="2.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <me>40 - 3 ( 2^3 + 2(6-4) )</me>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Begin with the innermost grouping symbols (the parentheses):
            <me>40 - 3 ( 2^3 + 2(2) )</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Next, evaluate the exponent inside the outer parentheses:
            <me>40 - 3 ( 8 + 2(2) )</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Perform the multiplication inside the parentheses:
            <me>40 - 3 ( 8 + 4 )</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Finish simplifying inside the outer parentheses by adding:
            <me>40 - 3 ( 12 )</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Multiply before subtracting:
            <me>40 - 36</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, subtract to find the result:
            <me>4</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>

      <task workspace="3.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <me>5 + \frac{(12 - 4)^2}{2(4)}</me>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Treat the fraction as a grouping symbol, simplifying its numerator and denominator first. Start with the parentheses in the numerator:
            <me>5 + \frac{(8)^2}{2(4)}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Evaluate the exponent in the numerator:
            <me>5 + \frac{64}{2(4)}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Multiply in the denominator:
            <me>5 + \frac{64}{8}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Perform the division indicated by the fraction bar before adding:
            <me>5 + 8</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, add to get the final answer:
            <me>13</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>

<task workspace="3.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <me>\frac{2(5^2 - 1)}{3(4+2) - 6}</me>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Treat the entire numerator and the entire denominator as separate groups to simplify first.
          </p>
          <p>
            In the numerator, begin with the exponent inside the parentheses. In the denominator, start with the addition inside the parentheses:
            <me>\frac{2(25 - 1)}{3(6) - 6}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Continue simplifying the groups. Subtract inside the numerator's parentheses and multiply in the denominator:
            <me>\frac{2(24)}{18 - 6}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Finish simplifying both the numerator and denominator:
            <me>\frac{48}{12}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, divide:
            <me>4</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>

      <task workspace="3.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <me>100 - \left( 2^4 + \frac{3(8-2)}{4-1} \right)</me>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Begin with the innermost grouping symbols. Here, the fraction inside the parentheses acts as its own group. Start by simplifying the numerator and denominator of that fraction:
            <me>100 - \left( 2^4 + \frac{3(6)}{3} \right)</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Multiply in the numerator of the fraction:
            <me>100 - \left( 2^4 + \frac{18}{3} \right)</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Divide to resolve the fraction entirely:
            <me>100 - ( 2^4 + 6 )</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Next, evaluate the exponent inside the remaining parentheses:
            <me>100 - ( 16 + 6 )</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Add inside the parentheses:
            <me>100 - 22</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, subtract to find the result:
            <me>78</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>

    </exercise>

</worksheet>